5. DATA, LICENSE VALIDATION AND UPDATES
   License check. The Software communicates with dbexpert.ai services
   automatically to validate license keys, to register (pair) an
   installation with a dbexpert.ai account, and to obtain updates. For
   this it sends the license key and the installation's host name,
   machine identifier, operating system and version. If you obtain a
   community key by a code sent to your e-mail address, it also sends
   that e-mail address. The license check sends nothing about the
   database servers the Software monitors.

   Online monitor. Sending monitoring results to a dbexpert.ai account
   (the "online monitor") is OFF unless an administrator of the
   installation turns it on in the product, and an administrator can
   turn it off again at any time. While it is on, the Software sends the
   account the results of its analysis of the database servers it
   monitors: findings, counts, severities, detection results,
   performance metrics and forecasts. Before anything is sent, server
   addresses, logins and object names are replaced by stand-ins that the
   installation generates; SQL is sent only as its shape, without the
   values in it; and anything the Software does not recognize as safe to
   send is withheld. The real names behind the stand-ins stay on the
   installation. If the administrator enters the address of the
   installation's console, that address is sent as well, so that the
   online monitor can link back to the installation. Turning the online
   monitor off stops this sending. It does not delete information
   already sent.

   Database credentials you configure are stored locally and are never
   transmitted to dbexpert.ai.
   The local language model runs on your system; prompts and findings
   processed by it are not sent to dbexpert.ai or any model vendor.

